WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Six Sigma Deployment Planning Matrix

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.233
Welcome to this session on deployment planning.

3
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:11.933
What we’re going to look at now are ten key plans

4
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:18.966
that we need to develop to successfully rollout Six Sigma across an organization.

5
00:00:18.966 --> 00:00:29.132
The first is we need a metrics plan. This metrics plan is critical.

6
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:32.466
The metrics plan is basically defining

7
00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:36.699
what we’re going to measure? How we’re going to measure it? When we’re going to measure it,

8
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.833
at the business, operations, and process level?

9
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:44.966
We need a communications plan. How are we going to communicate with each other?

10
00:00:44.966 --> 00:00:48.966
What we’re going to communicate? How we are going to communicate?

11
00:00:48.966 --> 00:00:54.032
We need a budgeting plan that defines how budgeting will occur.

12
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.399
How much each thing will be allocated? A training plan.

13
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.700
Very simple. What are we going to train in? When we are going to train?

14
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.866
Who are we going to train? Accounting. We need an accounting plan.

15
00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.866
Because remember, our accounting system likely will not be able to keep track

16
00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:17.032
of a lot of the different costs and benefits associated with Six Sigma.

17
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:21.033
We need a goal setting plan. How do we set our goals

18
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:26.766
at the business, operation, and process level to ensure they are interrelated and correlated?

19
00:01:26.766 --> 00:01:31.532
We need a reward and recognition plan for when people do well.

20
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:35.699
How are we going to reward them. How do we reward our champions,

21
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.766
our black belts, our green belts, our white belts?

22
00:01:38.766 --> 00:01:45.666
How do we reward individual contributors that steps out of their lucrative well-defined career

23
00:01:45.666 --> 00:01:50.799
to pick up a leadership role for the company in this newfangled thing called Six Sigma?

24
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.900
If they're successful, what are we going to do for them?

25
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.466
They need to have the answer to the question, what's in it for me?

26
00:01:57.466 --> 00:02:03.132
We need a rewards plan. How are they going to be rewarded?

27
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:07.399
What are they going to be rewarded? See, that's a little different than recognize.

28
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.733
Project planning. How and when are projects identified, selected, and so on?

29
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.933
We need a retention plan for our key players.

30
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:20.766
Hey, once they got that knowledge how do you stop them from leaving the company?

31
00:02:20.766 --> 00:02:23.732
If you don't take care of them, they’re gone.

32
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:27.733
So, how are you going to retain a lot of these key players?

33
00:02:27.733 --> 00:02:33.433
So, those are the ten fundamental plans that must be in place.

34
00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:39.233
Now, very briefly. I’ll fold this over.

35
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:46.199
Here are the planning elements. I’ll use metrics, as an example.

36
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:53.166
Our planning needs to consider, how are we going to define the metrics?

37
00:02:53.166 --> 00:02:56.399
How do we define our metrics?

38
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.066
How do we document the way those metrics are calculated?

39
00:03:01.066 --> 00:03:05.799
For example, I once discovered in one company, after we got the rollout going.

40
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.533
That there was about six different ways that the moving average was being calculated,

41
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:14.733
for one of the key operations metrics.

42
00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:17.766
So, everybody kept turning in different results.

43
00:03:17.766 --> 00:03:21.766
That's because we didn't have the method of calculation documented

44
00:03:21.766 --> 00:03:24.999
for the key metrics that were going to be used.

45
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.933
So, you got to define what the metrics are. You gotta document them

46
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:35.066
on how they're calculated, and so on. Then we have to execute those metrics.

47
00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:41.066
How are they executed? How are they implemented into the organization?

48
00:03:41.066 --> 00:03:44.832
Are they implemented with learning curves and so on?

49
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:49.066
What's their goals? Is there a goal aligned with them?

50
00:03:49.066 --> 00:03:53.066
Then, how are those metrics tracked and reported?

51
00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:59.699
That's so very key. Is it reported weekly, quarterly, monthly, biannually, semiannually, etc.

52
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.933
And finally, how do we validate

53
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:10.066
not only the performance of those metrics, the validity and reliability of those metrics?

54
00:04:10.066 --> 00:04:13.032
Do the metrics measure, what they purport to measure?

55
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:16.066
See there's a lot of things to consider.

56
00:04:16.066 --> 00:04:23.099
A lot of planning elements that need to be addressed in a metrics plan.

57
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.966
And we will talk about these others as well.

58
00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:31.966
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
